        '          '#39'Inhabits rocky areas along shallow exposed coast line' +
        '.'#39'#10#10'#39'Greenlin'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'gs can change their color to blend with the surroundi' +
        'ng sunlit r'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'ock and seaweed.  Their scales are very rough and giv' +
        'e the body '#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'a sandpaper-like texture.'#39'#10#10'#39'An 1886 description ' +
        'of a greenling co'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'mes from naturalist J.K. Lord.  He was overcome by it' +
        's beauty, a'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'nd said "its sides...rival in beauty many a tropical ' +
        'flower...[a'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'nd are] adorned with colors not only conspicuous for ' +
        'their brill'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'iancy, but grouped and blended in a manner one sees o' +
        'nly represe'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'nted in the plumage of a bird, the wing of a butterfl' +
        'y, or the p'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'etals of an orchid...red, blue, orange, and green are' +
        ' so mingled'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39' that the only thing I can think of as a comparison i' +
        's a floatin'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39'g flower bed, and even the gardener'#39'#39'#39's art, in gro' +
        'uping, is but a'#39' +'
      
        '          '#39' bungle contrasted with nature'#39'#39'#39's painting."'#39'#10#1' +
        '0'#39'Edibility is good.'#39'#10 +'
      
        '          #10'#39'Range is from the Bering Sea to Southern Californi' +
        'a.'#39'#10'
